er for Resources and Climate Change","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Full Council","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Pension Board","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Lead Member for Resources and Climate Change","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"SBSA - Audit and Governance Committee","time":"","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Health Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"People Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Investment management is delivering above-benchmark returns despite cash position constraints, demonstrating competent treasury execution under stress.","lead":"Strong treasury management during a period of cash depletion and liquidity pressure demonstrates financial discipline and capability, which supports lender confidence in the council's ability to manage large external borrowing programmes.","iso":"2026-09-29","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council expects to require \u00a3136.558m in new external borrowing during 2026/27, to be raised after exhausting \u00a375.000m of internal resources.","lead":"A borrowing requirement of this scale and imminence will determine the council's market access and cost of capital, directly affecting whether it can proceed with capital plans; the wording about timing suggests borrowing may be deferred if rates remain high.","iso":"2026-09-29","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Capital programme costs have increased above original forecast, raising total external borrowing requirement by \u00a32.136m to \u00a3211.558m.","lead":"Cost overruns mean larger funding gaps; combined with depleting cash reserves, the council has less flexibility to absorb further programme pressures or delays in accessing debt markets.","iso":"2026-09-29","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","headline":"Investment management is delivering above-benchmark returns despite cash position constraints, demonstrating competent treasury execution under stress.","detail":"Q1 average investment return of 4.01% exceeded the SONIA benchmark of 3.73% by 28 basis points. Investment risk exposure (0.004\u20130.005%) remains far below the strategy's allowable limit of 0.050%, indicating disciplined credit quality management. This performance is achieved while managing a falling and constrained portfolio.","category":"governance","matters":"Strong treasury management during a period of cash depletion and liquidity pressure demonstrates financial discipline and capability, which supports lender confidence in the council's ability to manage large external borrowing programmes.","figures":"Investment return 4.01% vs 3.73% SONIA (+28 basis points); investment risk 0.004\u20130.005% vs 0.050% limit","quote":"The average investment return over Q1 was 4.01% performing above the benchmark rate by 28 basis points (or 0.28 percentage points).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","headline":"Council expects to require \u00a3136.558m in new external borrowing during 2026/27, to be raised after exhausting \u00a375.000m of internal resources.","detail":"The strategy allocates \u00a375.000m of internal borrowing (reserves and temporary working capital) towards the capital and capitalisation programmes. This leaves \u00a3136.558m that must be raised from external debt markets. The timing of this borrowing will be managed in relation to treasury strategy and economic conditions.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A borrowing requirement of this scale and imminence will determine the council's market access and cost of capital, directly affecting whether it can proceed with capital plans; the wording about timing suggests borrowing may be deferred if rates remain high.","figures":"\u00a3136.558m new external borrowing; \u00a375.000m internal borrowing; \u00a3211.558m total 2026/27 borrowing requirement","quote":"It is therefore expected that new borrowing of \u00a3136.558m may be required to support the capital programme and capitalisation direction during 2026/27, although the timing of actual borrowing will be considered in the context of the wider treasury management position and underlying economic environment.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","headline":"Capital programme costs have increased above original forecast, raising total external borrowing requirement by \u00a32.136m to \u00a3211.558m.","detail":"Capital programme CFR closing balance revised upward from \u00a3334.064m to \u00a3339.700m. Capital programme borrowing requirement increased from \u00a330.008m original estimate to \u00a333.344m revised forecast. Total new borrowing requirement now \u00a3211.558m against original estimate of \u00a3209.422m.","category":"capital","matters":"Cost overruns mean larger funding gaps; combined with depleting cash reserves, the council has less flexibility to absorb further programme pressures or delays in accessing debt markets.","figures":"Capital programme CFR \u00a3334.064m to \u00a3339.700m; capital borrowing requirement \u00a330.008m to \u00a333.344m; total borrowing need \u00a3211.558m vs \u00a3209.422m forecast","quote":"The total CFR is expected to give rise to a new borrowing requirement of \u00a3211.558m (\u00a3141.558m capital programme and \u00a370.000m capitalisation direction) by the end of the year, compared to the original estimate of requirement of \u00a3209.422m (\u00a3139.422m capital programme and \u00a370.000m capitalisation direction).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 2 TM Prudential Indicators - 29.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79299/79299.pdf","headline":"Cash investment balances have fallen 52% in one year to \u00a365.5m as the council uses reserves to fund capital and revenue spending while delaying new borrowing.","detail":"Balances fell from \u00a3137.2m in Q1 2025/26 to \u00a365.5m in Q1 2026/27. The council is using cash reserves to support capital programme and revenue spending rather than accessing debt markets, a deliberate strategy given current interest rates. A \u00a32.0m PWLB loan at 7.125% matured during the quarter.","category":"reserves","matters":"Rapidly shrinking liquidity cushion means the council will be forced to enter debt markets for substantial sums within months, reducing flexibility to time borrowing decisions or manage market windows.","figures":"\u00a3137.2m (Q1 2025/26) to \u00a365.5m (Q1 2026/27), 52% reduction; \u00a32.0m PWLB maturing at 7.125%","quote":"Cash investment balances as at 30 June 2026 have fallen by 52% in one year, from \u00a3137.2m at Q1 2025/26 to \u00a365.5m due to continued use of cash balances to support the capital programme and revenue cashflow requirements whilst new borrowing is delayed during a time of elevated rates.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Cabinet Cover Report PTBs and ITT","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s79295/79295.pdf","headline":"Uptake of the council's cost-mitigation schemes remains limited despite available benefits.","detail":"The paper identifies that uptake of Personal Travel Budgets and Independent Travel Training is limited due to awareness barriers. Many families remain unaware of the full range of support available or how it can be adapted to their circumstances. The council is attempting to address this through improved communications and stakeholder engagement.","category":"savings","matters":"If cost management depends on increasing uptake of alternative schemes to offset rising demand, but current uptake is limited and awareness barriers persist, the strategy may not deliver expected savings.","figures":"No uptake figures provided","quote":"despite these benefits, uptake of Personal Travel Budgets and Independent Travel Training was limited due to a number of barriers, and that although existing policies provide flexibility, these options were not widely understood.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-10"},"name":"East Sussex CC","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"Authority's reported \u00a30.6m underspend is entirely dependent on unbudgeted grant income and interest income; operational position is \u00a30.6m overspend","lead":"The authority's apparent financial health depends entirely on temporary government grants that may not continue and interest income that will decline with falling rates; the true operational position shows material overspend","also":["8 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Place Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-23","n_findings":9,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Agenda Item 5 - Quarter 1 Revenue Budget Monitoring","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.eastsussex.gov.uk/documents/s78854/78854.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":92,"news_drafts_total":3}}